course: dictionary
lesson: 3628
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word fine has many meanings, so context matters most. Read the words around it to find which meaning fits. «I'm fine, thanks» means you are well. «Fine weather» means sunny and dry. The clues tell you which one the writer means.
explain: Swap a meaning into the sentence to check it. «A short time alone is fine at 9 or 10 if it's short, a grown-up is close by, and there is a plan.» Does acceptable, all right fit? Yes, it does. Fine means all right here.
explain: Sometimes fine is a noun, a thing you must pay. «A girl in Wales called Ella borrowed a library book and lost it; the fine and the cost of the book came out of her savings.» Money paid because a rule was broken is a fine.
fact: Fine has many meanings, so always check the words around it.
fact: «Fine» can mean well or healthy, as in «I'm fine, thanks.»
fact: «Fine» can mean acceptable or all right, as in «That's fine.»
fact: A fine is money you must pay as a punishment for breaking a rule, as in «a parking fine.»
fact: «Fine sand» means sand with tiny grains, and «fine hair» means thin, soft hair.
exercise: «A short time alone is fine at 9 or 10 if it's short, a grown-up is close by, and there is a plan.» What does fine mean here? | Of high quality | Very thin or tiny | *Acceptable, all right | Sunny and dry
exercise: «Ella lost the library book, and the fine came out of her savings.» What is a fine? | A bright, sunny day | A tiny grain of sand | A kind of healthy food | *Money you must pay as a punishment
exercise: «The sand was so fine that it ran through my fingers.» What does fine mean in this sentence? | Sunny and dry | Well and healthy | *Very thin or tiny | Good enough to pass a test
test: «Fledglings on the ground are usually fine and their parents nearby, so it's best to leave them.» What does fine mean here? | A punishment paid in money | Very thin | *All right, safe | Sunny and dry
test: «The people behind the robocall were fined.» What does fined mean here? | Given a prize for being clever | Made very tiny | Asked to sing a song | *Made to pay money as a punishment
exam: Which sentence uses fine as a noun, meaning money paid as a punishment? | Fine weather today | That's fine | I'm fine, thanks | *The parking fine was paid by Ella
